1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:04,287
Could you make it all the more real for us in terms of some

2
00:00:04,287 --> 00:00:08,376
examples of common places where courageous acts make all the

3
00:00:08,376 --> 00:00:11,980
difference at work at -- or where people often shy away?

4
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:14,764
Kind of -- what specific, kinds of, moments are we talking about here?

5
00:00:14,764 --> 00:00:18,170
So there are few, sort of, prototypical types of acts

6
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:19,464
that if you sample,

7
00:00:19,464 --> 00:00:22,707
you know, thousands of people, as I have, say,

8
00:00:22,707 --> 00:00:27,284
75% or more will say, yeah, unfortunately these behaviors are

9
00:00:27,284 --> 00:00:29,573
moderately courageous or more.

10
00:00:29,573 --> 00:00:34,092
The most obvious type of behavior -- set of behaviors are what I call

11
00:00:34,092 --> 00:00:35,760
truth to power behavior.

12
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,991
So these are challenging your boss or skip level bosses.

13
00:00:38,991 --> 00:00:42,120
You know, it could be about policies or practices.

14
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:46,277
It could be about interpersonal behaviors that are offensive

15
00:00:46,277 --> 00:00:47,052
or hurtful.

16
00:00:47,052 --> 00:00:50,473
It could be about actually illegal or unethical things.

17
00:00:50,473 --> 00:00:53,956
It can be about going to bat for your own subordinates

18
00:00:53,956 --> 00:00:55,270
to people above you.

19
00:00:55,270 --> 00:00:57,394
So lots of truth to power behaviors.

20
00:00:57,394 --> 00:00:58,607
Somewhat surprising

21
00:00:58,607 --> 00:01:02,362
going back to that conversation, I was surprised the degree to

22
00:01:02,362 --> 00:01:06,364
which when I just asked people, you know, tell me about a behavior

23
00:01:06,364 --> 00:01:08,395
at work that would be courageous.

24
00:01:08,395 --> 00:01:11,786
I expected that everybody would say truth to power type behaviors.

25
00:01:11,786 --> 00:01:16,557
What I wasn't prepared for was the frequency of people talking about

26
00:01:16,557 --> 00:01:20,901
how hard it was to have honest conversation with peers or even

27
00:01:20,901 --> 00:01:24,533
have honest conversations or give difficult feedback

28
00:01:24,533 --> 00:01:25,672
to subordinates.

29
00:01:25,672 --> 00:01:29,457
And the reason I think that was originally surprising to me is I

30
00:01:29,457 --> 00:01:33,062
was thinking primarily of risk in terms of economic or career

31
00:01:33,062 --> 00:01:33,843
consequences.

32
00:01:33,843 --> 00:01:34,223
Right.

33
00:01:34,223 --> 00:01:36,970
If it doesn't go well, my promotion, my pay,

34
00:01:36,970 --> 00:01:38,828
my future here is at stake.

35
00:01:38,828 --> 00:01:41,890
It turns out people have a few fundamental fears,

36
00:01:41,890 --> 00:01:43,800
and that's only one of them.

37
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:47,762
People are also highly afraid of social consequences.

38
00:01:47,762 --> 00:01:49,088
If you think about it, it makes sense,

39
00:01:49,088 --> 00:01:49,352
right?

40
00:01:49,352 --> 00:01:53,530
We evolved in small clans, bands, tribes,

41
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:56,145
and our daily task was survival.

42
00:01:56,145 --> 00:01:58,450
And if you got ostracized from your group,

43
00:01:58,450 --> 00:02:01,550
you were gonna die and you were going to die in short order.

44
00:02:01,550 --> 00:02:04,988
And so it's not illogical that even though that's not our

45
00:02:04,988 --> 00:02:07,690
environment today, evolutionarily we're still

46
00:02:07,690 --> 00:02:10,760
programmed to be hugely afraid of being ostracized,

47
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:12,802
to have social consequences.

48
00:02:12,802 --> 00:02:15,110
We also hate psychological risks.

49
00:02:15,110 --> 00:02:18,715
You know, we don't -- if you say, why don't people step up and try a

50
00:02:18,715 --> 00:02:21,418
new task or take a new job or be more innovative?

51
00:02:21,418 --> 00:02:24,949
The answer there is often they don't want to look stupid.

52
00:02:24,949 --> 00:02:26,730
They don't want to feel embarrassed.

53
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:29,430
They don't want to see self-doubt creep in.

54
00:02:29,430 --> 00:02:33,415
And so there's actually this huge range of behaviors that's not just

55
00:02:33,415 --> 00:02:34,842
about challenging power.

56
00:02:34,842 --> 00:02:38,900
It's about difficult interpersonal situations with peers,

57
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:41,197
subordinates, external partners.

58
00:02:41,197 --> 00:02:42,736
It's about being innovative.

59
00:02:42,736 --> 00:02:46,880
Yeah. I developed an index of the most common behaviors I heard

60
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:50,556
about from thousands of people, and there's 35 different

61
00:02:50,556 --> 00:02:51,224
behaviors.

62
00:02:51,224 --> 00:02:53,790
And many of them you would probably say,

63
00:02:53,790 --> 00:02:56,580
gosh, for a professional or a manager,

64
00:02:56,580 --> 00:02:58,539
isn't that just doing your job?

65
00:02:58,539 --> 00:03:02,440
And I'd say, yeah, it is, but these things happen

66
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:04,553
surprisingly infrequently.

67
00:03:04,553 --> 00:03:06,111
Okay, well, I love this.

68
00:03:06,111 --> 00:03:09,575
So categories of fears, economic risk might lose my job or

69
00:03:09,575 --> 00:03:10,729
money or promotion.

70
00:03:10,729 --> 00:03:15,221
Social risk, folks will not like me, shut me, ostracize me.

71
00:03:15,221 --> 00:03:16,400
Psychological risk,

72
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:18,048
I might feel stupid or embarrassed if I,

73
00:03:18,048 --> 00:03:20,956
you know, screw this up and look real dumb or those, kind of,

74
00:03:20,956 --> 00:03:22,009
the three categories.

75
00:03:22,009 --> 00:03:23,046
Are there some more there?

76
00:03:23,046 --> 00:03:26,724
Well, the fourth one, which is real in many contexts

77
00:03:26,724 --> 00:03:28,887
I didn't mention, is physical.

78
00:03:28,887 --> 00:03:31,296
If you go back 2,000 years, write of courage,

79
00:03:31,296 --> 00:03:33,828
writing the vast history of courage,

80
00:03:33,828 --> 00:03:36,405
writing was about military contexts.

81
00:03:36,405 --> 00:03:40,692
And yeah, sure enough, there are still in military

82
00:03:40,692 --> 00:03:41,829
firefighting.

83
00:03:41,829 --> 00:03:45,550
You know, police work plenty of other settings that come to mind,

84
00:03:45,550 --> 00:03:47,550
there are still physical risks.

85
00:03:47,550 --> 00:03:51,316
And even frankly, I was surprised the degree to

86
00:03:51,316 --> 00:03:56,637
which folks who work in any, sort of, service occupation and bartenders,

87
00:03:56,637 --> 00:04:02,123
waiters, customer service, actually report cases of being physically

88
00:04:02,123 --> 00:04:05,863
assaulted, accosted, threatened with a weapon.

89
00:04:05,863 --> 00:04:08,850
So there's physical risk also that some people face.

90
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:11,850


